config UM_BACKEND_KVM_V2
	bool "KVM backend v2 (production)"
	depends on X86_64
	default n
	help
	  The v2 KVM backend reimplemented from scratch on top of the
	  12 ARCH=um core refactors landed during the v1 → v2 transition
	  (see Documentation/virt/uml/redesign/02-workstreams/D-kvm-backend/
	  25-v2-restart-guide.md). Uses TDP via per-host-CPU vCPU pool,
	  KVM_SET_USER_MEMORY_REGION memslot covering uml_physmem, and
	  standard x86_64 memory layout (uml_physmem in PML4[256+]).

	  At the v2 substrate parity milestone (memo 26 §H.1b,
	  2026-04-30) v2 is at full seccomp parity on the regrtest-
	  substrate gate (PASS=25 / FAIL=3 / EXPECTED_FAIL=3, bit-for-
	  bit match) and ~2× faster than seccomp on minimal Python
	  startup. Phase A through Phase F have closed; Phase G (SMP),
	  Phase H (perf optimization beyond the current 2× lead),
	  Phase I (this Kconfig + KUnit/ftrace/docs polish), and
	  Phase J (validation) are still open. Multi-thread + multi-
	  subinterpreter workloads (cpython-parity test_struct's
	  test_endian_table_init_subinterpreters) show a ~50% flake
	  rate that's deferred to a focused investigation session
	  (memo §H.1b residual).

	  Boot uses seccomp via DYNAMIC unless `backend=force=kvm-v2`
	  explicitly picks v2. Selecting Y here builds v2's machinery
	  alongside seccomp; the runtime backend is still chosen by
	  cmdline / capability probe.

	  The archived v1 implementation lives at
	  arch/um/backend/kvm-v1-archive/ (not built; depends on BROKEN);
	  memos 21-24 document its history.

	  Say Y to build v2 alongside seccomp; production runtimes can
	  either default to seccomp or opt into v2 via the cmdline
	  selector. Phase J validation (24h continuous + Tier 1/2/3 +
	  soak) will gate flipping `default y`.

config UM_BACKEND_KVM_V2_GADGET
	bool "Stay-in-guest LSTAR gadget for trivial syscalls"
	depends on UM_BACKEND_KVM_V2
	default y
	help
	  Phase H gadget mechanism: an in-LSTAR dispatch tree that handles
	  trivial syscalls (getpid, gettid, getppid, getuid, geteuid, getgid,
	  getegid, getcpu, time, clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC)) entirely
	  in-guest by reading per-task fields via swapgs + %gs:OFFSET. Avoids
	  the host-side KVM_EXIT_IO roundtrip for those syscalls.

	  Performance impact: bench-micro getpid drops from ~36 800 cyc/call
	  to ~90 cyc/call (~410× faster) on Zen 4. Workload-level speedup
	  (Python imports, anything calling clock_gettime in a tight loop) is
	  proportional to gadget-syscall density.

	  Mechanism: the LSTAR target is a ~343-byte trampoline page mapped
	  in the kernel-half VA range. On guest SYSCALL the CPU jumps there
	  at CPL=0; the body decodes RAX (NR), reads task state from a
	  per-vCPU state page (programmed via MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE), and
	  returns via SYSRETQ — no vmexit. Any non-gadget NR falls through
	  to the same out+sysretq fallback path that the non-gadget v2
	  backend uses, routed through KVM_EXIT_IO → handle_io_trap →
	  handle_syscall.

	  Selecting N disables the gadget at compile time:
	    - trampoline_install writes only the 5-byte fallback LSTAR
	    - per-vCPU state pages are not allocated
	    - load_user_sregs skips the per-dispatch field refresh
	    - MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE is not programmed
	  The result is structurally identical to pre-gadget v2: every
	  SYSCALL takes the slow KVM_EXIT_IO path.

	  Say N for security-sensitive deployments that don't want a custom
	  CPL=0 trampoline body executing in the guest. Say Y (default) to
	  pay the trampoline complexity in exchange for the perf benefit.

config UM_BACKEND_KVM_V2_KUNIT
	tristate "KVM v2 backend KUnit tests"
	depends on UM_BACKEND_KVM_V2 && KUNIT
	default n
	help
	  Build the v2 backend KUnit suites: marshal-shape (D.2 +
	  task #74) and any future per-helper tests. These are pure
	  data-transform tests — no host syscalls, no vCPU state.
	  Catches off-by-one register slot mappings + RFLAGS-bit-1
	  invariants in kvm_v2_marshal_to/from_kvm_regs.

	  Say N for production builds; Y or M to validate marshal
	  correctness during v2 development.

config UM_BACKEND_KVM_V2_STATE_TRACE
	bool "KVM v2 state-snapshot trace ring (debugging only)"
	depends on UM_BACKEND_KVM_V2 && DEBUG_FS
	default n
	help
	  Per-CPU ring buffer that captures a structured snapshot of
	  ~30 v2 state fields (live KVM regs, sregs, per-task arch
	  state, per-mm tlb_gen, per-vCPU last_seen, IST frame) at
	  every operation transition the state-audit Layer 2
	  inventoried (Documentation/virt/uml/redesign/02-workstreams/
	  D-kvm-backend/state-audit/02-operations-inventory.md).

	  Designed to root-cause cross-task state contamination under
	  SMP T>=N stress (memo §G mt-mini residual). Per-(pid, op)
	  state evolution from a FAIL boot is dumped to dmesg via
	  debugfs or via the magic ioport 0xfd from user space.

	  Three layers of shielding from production paths:
	    1. CONFIG=n compiles every hook to do{}while(0) — zero
	       text, zero icache footprint.
	    2. CONFIG=y but disabled at runtime: each hook is a single
	       5-byte NOP via static_branch_unlikely.
	    3. Per-CPU ring storage; no cross-CPU cacheline traffic.

	  Toggle at runtime:
	    echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/um_kvm_v2_trace/enabled
	    # ... reproduce ...
	    echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/um_kvm_v2_trace/dump
	    dmesg | grep KVMV2T

	  Or from a user-space test binary on FAIL:
	    asm volatile("outb %%al, $0xfd" : : "a"(0));

	  Default ring size is 2 MB / CPU (≈5400 entries × ~384 B).
	  Adjustable via /sys/kernel/debug/um_kvm_v2_trace/ring_bytes
	  (read at next enable=1).

	  Say N for normal builds (zero overhead). Say Y when
	  diagnosing v2 SMP races; the runtime is gated on debugfs
	  toggle so you can ship a CONFIG=y kernel and only pay cost
	  when actively tracing.

config UM_BACKEND_KVM_V2_RDPMC
	bool "Allow guest userspace rdpmc (CR4.PCE=1)"
	depends on UM_BACKEND_KVM_V2
	default y
	help
	  Set CR4.PCE=1 in the guest's per-vCPU control registers so
	  userspace can execute `rdpmc` directly without #GP at CPL=3.
	  Without this, rdpmc is privileged (PMC accessible only to
	  guest CPL=0 code — for UML that means the LSTAR gadget and
	  exception stubs, neither of which currently calls rdpmc).

	  What this enables:
	    - Userspace `perf` inside the guest gets accurate hardware
	      counter reads (instructions retired, cycles, branches,
	      cache misses, ...) directly via rdpmc.  No gadget syscall
	      round-trip, no IPC, just a one-instruction read.
	    - profile-guided JITs (PyPy, JS engines) and microbenchmark
	      harnesses can self-measure inside the UML guest.
	    - The example at
	      Documentation/virt/uml/examples/rdpmc/ demonstrates the
	      pattern (read PMC fixed-counter 0 = instructions retired,
	      assert it advances across a busy loop).

	  What this does NOT enable:
	    - rdmsr / wrmsr on PMU MSRs — those stay privileged.  The
	      guest cannot program PERFEVTSEL counters from userspace
	      (counter selection is a kernel-only operation under
	      Linux's perf subsystem; gated behind the perf_event
	      syscall).
	    - Specific PMU event allowlist control — that's a separate
	      KVM cap (KVM_CAP_PMU_EVENT_FILTER) not exposed here.
	    - Crossing the host/guest counter-attribution boundary in
	      any new way.  KVM's vPMU already virtualizes counters
	      per-vCPU; CR4.PCE just changes the CPL gate on rdpmc.

	  Cost: a single bit set in the sregs install path; zero
	  runtime overhead.

	  Security: rdpmc returns counter values for the running vCPU.
	  In SMP UML with vCPU pinning (SMP-T37) those values reflect
	  the host CPU the vCPU is bound to.  Treating them as a side
	  channel against other guests on the same physical CPU is a
	  known concern for any KVM userspace -- gate this OFF for
	  multi-tenant sandbox profiles.

	  Say Y for research/dev/CI builds.  Say N for sandbox
	  profiles that explicitly want rdpmc unavailable.

config UM_BACKEND_KVM_V2_APERFMPERF_PASSTHROUGH
	bool "Pass APERF/MPERF MSRs through to guest (host-counter visibility)"
	depends on UM_BACKEND_KVM_V2
	default n
	help
	  Issue KVM_ENABLE_CAP(KVM_CAP_X86_DISABLE_EXITS) with the
	  KVM_X86_DISABLE_EXITS_APERFMPERF bit during VM creation so the
	  guest can read IA32_APERF (0xE7) and IA32_MPERF (0xE8) directly
	  via rdmsr.  Without this, KVM traps the read and returns zero;
	  with it, the rdmsr passes through to hardware and the guest sees
	  the actual host performance counters for the CPU the vCPU is
	  currently pinned to.

	  Useful for:
	    - Guest-side frequency / utilization observation
	      (cpupower, turbostat, perf governors)
	    - Reproducing the QEMU/libvirt gap where -overcommit
	      cpu-pm=on plumbs HLT/MWAIT/PAUSE/CSTATE but not APERFMPERF
	      (the v2 backend is its own VMM and exposes the bit directly)
	    - Anyone studying how the disable-exits surface behaves under
	      a non-QEMU userspace

	  Host requirements:
	    - boot_cpu_has(X86_FEATURE_APERFMPERF) must be set; KVM's
	      kvm_get_allowed_disable_exits() gates the bit on that.
	      The probe is non-fatal — vm_create logs a warning and
	      continues if the host rejects the cap.

	  Constraints:
	    - The ioctl is issued from kvm_v2_vm_create(), strictly
	      before kvm_v2_vcpu_create().  KVM's KVM_CAP_X86_DISABLE_EXITS
	      handler enforces `if (kvm->created_vcpus) return -EINVAL;`
	      so the ordering must not slip.
	    - Bit is per-VM; in v2 there is one VM per UML invocation
	      (decisions-log D57) so this is also a per-instance toggle.

	  Runtime override:
	    Boot param  kvm_v2_aperfmperf=on   forces enable
	                kvm_v2_aperfmperf=off  forces disable
	    overriding the Kconfig default.  The override only takes
	    effect when CONFIG_UM_BACKEND_KVM_V2_APERFMPERF_PASSTHROUGH=y
	    (the cmdline parser is gated on the same symbol).

	  See Documentation/virt/uml/aperf-mperf.rst for the in-guest
	  rdmsr verification path and the QEMU bridging context.

	  Say N for production builds; Y when reproducing the QEMU
	  disable-exits gap or doing host-counter-visible workloads.

config UM_BACKEND_KVM_V2_ITIMER_VIRTUAL
	bool "Account host KVM_RUN CPU time as guest utime (ITIMER_VIRTUAL)"
	depends on UM_BACKEND_KVM_V2
	default y
	help
	  Account TSC-cycle delta around each ioctl(KVM_RUN) to the
	  calling task's utime via account_user_time().  Without this,
	  host timer signals interrupting KVM_RUN default to is_user=0
	  (system time) — guest utime never advances and ITIMER_VIRTUAL
	  never delivers SIGVTALRM.  SMP-T80, state-audit/29.

	  Cost: ~30 cycles per KVM_RUN dispatch (two rdtsc reads +
	  mult_frac conversion).  Negligible vs the original
	  clock_gettime-based approach which cost ~25 % bench-py
	  regression on Python startup.  Default y because the cost is
	  small enough not to need an opt-in.

	  Say N only if you specifically need to disable guest utime
	  accounting (rare).  setitimer(ITIMER_VIRTUAL) will arm but
	  never fire when N.
